WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.690
- [narrator] When Donald Trump
nominated Amy Coney Barrett

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:05.680
to replace Ruth Bader
Ginsburg on the Supreme court,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.150
Liberal Democrats scream bloody murder

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.460
about the Legitimacy
of appointing a justice

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.920
this close to an election
while calling attention

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.470
to Barrett's Catholicism
and anti abortion animus.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.320
- And it looks like having three
names is the only thing she

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.060
and her predecessor have in common.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:21.893
- [narrator] But according to

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:23.680
Reason Senior Editor Jacob Sullum,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.040
when it comes to criminal
justice and abortion,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.170
the two may have far more in common than

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.290
Conservatives and
Progressive seem to realize.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.710
- Ruth Bader Ginsburg was about as good

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.165
as you could reasonably expect a

00:00:37.165 --> 00:00:40.560
quote unquote Liberal Justice to be.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.610
She was generally sound
on the Fourth Amendment

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.040
on the first Amendment when it
came to issues of due process

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.047
and criminal justice cases in general.

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:51.790
[narrator] Despite being
attacked as a doctrinaire law

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.870
and order conservative Barrett says

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.790
Sullum has issued opinions that

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.450
track pretty well with Ginsburg

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.410
when it comes to limiting the police.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.440
He points to one ruling in
particular in which Barrett found

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.960
that DEA agents didn't have
a right to search a property

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.030
when granted permission by a
woman who didn't live there.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.900
- So therefore this was an invalid search

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.570
and therefore they can't use this evidence

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.790
against the drug suspect.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.840
That's an example of a
decision that that would be

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.860
traditionally condemned by conservatives,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.200
where you hear you're letting this guy

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.460
off on a technicality,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.060
but in fact it's what the fourth Amendment

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.560
in my view at least requires.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.800
So that's encouraging.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.320
[narrator] In at least one
case involving a detective who

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.420
lied in a statement so
that he could arrest an

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.220
innocent man for murder.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.640
Barrett came down hard on his
claim to qualified immunity

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.800
which shields law enforcement officers

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.120
from being sued for misconduct.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.180
- And Barrett took credit said no way.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.780
There's no way that you could have thought

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.800
it was okay to lie in a
probable cause affidavit.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.380
The guy that you victimized should

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.420
have a chance to make that case in court.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.980
- [narrator] When it comes to abortion.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.280
Barrett has said that
she thinks it's unlikely

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.100
that the court will fully
overturn Roe V. Wade,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.180
even as she has criticized the ruling as

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.080
an act of judicial overreach.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.120
Ruth Bader Ginsburg had
similar reservations about

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.900
the landmark 1973 case.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.380
- Ginsburg had two main
criticisms of Roe V. Wade.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.920
One was that she didn't think it took

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.640
the right analytical approach,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.260
instead of using this sort of vague notion

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.000
of privacy protected by
substantive due process

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.820
which really has no basis
in the constitution.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.300
She thought it should have been
an equal protection argument

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.180
that abortion laws discriminate
against people based on sex.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.120
And she also thought that the
Supreme court went too far

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.680
in preempting in a wholesale sort of way

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.160
state abortion laws.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.760
Ginsburg thought that this led to

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.900
an unnecessary federalization
of the issue that

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.660
led to a great deal of
division and acrimony.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.740
And if the court had left
a little bit more time

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.400
for the issue to work its way
through the democratic process

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.320
there wouldn't have been the
sense that the Supreme court

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.140
had arbitrarily overridden
the decision decisions of

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.260
State Legislators who are
trying to represent the

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.180
opinions of their Constituents.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.880
And I think Barrett would
agree with a lot of that.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.580
I honestly don't know how she's going to

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.300
approach abortion cases except to say that

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.720
she probably will be receptive
to upholding the sorts

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.260
of laws that are up to make their way to

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.200
the Supreme court,

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.920
which have to do not with wholesale bans

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.980
but with various kinds of restrictions.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.980
- [narrator] On issues such as gun rights

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.240
and federalism,

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.140
Barrett would likely be much better

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.280
from a libertarian perspective.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.540
- Ruth Bader Ginsburg, like
most people on the left

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.200
she had a blind spot when it
came to the second Amendment

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.400
which is after all part
of the bill of rights,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.000
she agreed with her other
Liberal colleagues that

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.180
the second Amendment basically
does not impose any limits

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.660
on what governments can do
in terms of gun control.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.160
The majority of course found otherwise

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.860
which I think was the right decision.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.980
She was also bad when it came

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.040
to Congresses use of the Commerce Clause.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.440
One was Obamacare where the issue was

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.860
can the federal government

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.620
under the pretext of regulating

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.760
interstate commerce say,

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.540
you must buy government
approved health insurance.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.840
And there were some very
compelling arguments

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.480
against that notion.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.260
This is not Interstate
Commerce for one thing.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.360
Barrett is definitely
going to be better when

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.100
it comes to the second Amendment.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.340
I suspect she will better when
it comes to property rights.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.840
I'm almost certain she's
going to be better in terms

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.240
of defining Congress's powers
under the Commerce Clause.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.000
So those are ways in which I
think she would definitely be

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.560
better from a libertarian perspective,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.100
on Fourth Amendment she looks pretty good.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.040
I can't say that she's
better than Ginsburg on

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.080
the Fourth Amendment.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.020
Ginsburg was good.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.160
But there are reasons to
think Barrett will be better

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.120
than Ginsburg on issues
that libertarians care about

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.020
and will be nearly as good or as good on

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.680
other issues that Progressives
tend to care about.

